UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 2: Show, don''t tell', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Show feelings through actions and details instead of naming the emotion directly.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. Rewrite \"Tom was angry\" using actions only.\nCOMPARE: Example: \"Tom slammed his pencil onto the desk and stared at the torn page.\" The actions imply anger.\n2. Write two clues that could show worry at a bus stop.\nCOMPARE: Checking the timetable twice and biting a lip are useful clues; naming the bus colour is not.\n3. Trim a draft that lists five adjectives for surprise into two clear sentences.\nCOMPARE: Choose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence.\n4. Improve a scene that could mean either surprise or fear.\nCOMPARE: Add a clue that distinguishes them, such as a jump back for fear versus a wide grin for pleasant surprise.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nWrite three short original scenes (two sentences each) that show worry, surprise and relief without using those three words. Underline the exact actions that suggest each feeling and explain one choice.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Show feelings through actions and details instead of naming the emotion directly.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance: Accept any clear action-based clues; reject drafts that merely rename the emotion. Require underlining or listing of the evidence actions. Accept plausible original solutions where the text supports them; require the learner to explain at least one independent choice.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: \"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without using that word.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Piling synonyms for the emotion is still telling. Correction: Choose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence.\n\nParent: Assess comprehension and language rather than penalising speech or motor difficulties unrelated to the objective. Keep written models out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Telling names the emotion; showing lets the reader infer it from actions, body language and surroundings. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Telling names the emotion; showing lets the reader infer it from actions, body language and surroundings.","Replacing angry with mad still tells; show observable evidence.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Name the feeling, then remove the label\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Not every detail helps. Choose actions a reader can picture that point towards one feeling. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Not every detail helps. Choose actions a reader can picture that point towards one feeling.","A smile alone can mean many feelings; add a second confirming clue.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Choose useful sensory or physical clues\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Two or three precise sentences are stronger than a long list of adjectives. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Two or three precise sentences are stronger than a long list of adjectives.","Piling synonyms for the emotion is still telling.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Keep the scene short and clear\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Read your scene aloud and ask what feeling seems most likely. If several feelings fit equally, add one clearer clue. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Read your scene aloud and ask what feeling seems most likely. If several feelings fit equally, add one clearer clue.","Do not claim the feeling is obvious if your clues support more than one reading.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Check a reader can infer the intended feeling\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Example: \"Tom slammed his pencil onto the desk and stared at the torn page.\" The actions imply anger. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Example: \"Tom slammed his pencil onto the desk and stared at the torn page.\" The actions imply anger.","Replacing angry with mad still tells; show observable evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Rewrite \"Tom was angry\" using actions only.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Checking the timetable twice and biting a lip are useful clues; naming the bus colour is not. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Checking the timetable twice and biting a lip are useful clues; naming the bus colour is not.","A smile alone can mean many feelings; add a second confirming clue.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Write two clues that could show worry at a bus stop.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Choose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Choose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence.","Piling synonyms for the emotion is still telling.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Trim a draft that lists five adjectives for surprise into two clear sentences.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Add a clue that distinguishes them, such as a jump back for fear versus a wide grin for pleasant surprise. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["Add a clue that distinguishes them, such as a jump back for fear versus a wide grin for pleasant surprise.","Do not claim the feeling is obvious if your clues support more than one reading.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Improve a scene that could mean either surprise or fear.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Meaningful understanding is shown when a learner transfers the method to new text or speech and explains their evidence.","marking_guide":"Require an original relevant example, accurate terminology and an explanation grounded in the example.","question":"Use your own example to demonstrate showing and explain the evidence supporting your choice.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: \"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without using that word.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"Explaining a correction helps the learner distinguish an effective strategy from a plausible-sounding but inaccurate approach.","marking_guide":"Require a genuine relevant error, accurate correction and a reason that refers to the target concept.","question":"Identify and correct a common mistake relating to clue in this lesson.","sample_answer":"Common error: Piling synonyms for the emotion is still telling. Correction: Choose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Submit your original independent work and your brief explanation of the key choices you made.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w04-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; compare the existing Coming Soon card and official syllabus content points before marking verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise showing.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Your goal is: Show feelings through actions and details instead of naming the emotion directly."],"guided":["Attempt each guided prompt without looking ahead.","Compare your attempt with the supplied specific feedback.","Guided comparison: Checking the timetable twice and biting a lip are useful clues; naming the bus colour is not."],"learn":["Study the first worked demonstration carefully.","Name the exact words or events that make it work.","Demonstration: \"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without using that word."],"quiz":["Read the whole question and eliminate misconceptions.","Prefer the option that quotes or uses actual evidence.","Likely trap: Piling synonyms for the emotion is still telling."],"submit":["Include your own response and a short reason.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation before sending.","Success looks like: Show feelings through actions and details instead of naming the emotion directly."],"teach0":["Focus on name the feeling, then remove the label.","Hold this teaching point: Telling names the emotion; showing lets the reader infer it from actions, body language and surroundings.","Worked comparison: Example: \"Tom slammed his pencil onto the desk and stared at the torn page.\" The actions imply anger."],"teach1":["Focus on choose useful sensory or physical clues.","Hold this teaching point: Not every detail helps. Choose actions a reader can picture that point towards one feeling.","Worked comparison: Checking the timetable twice and biting a lip are useful clues; naming the bus colour is not."],"teach2":["Focus on keep the scene short and clear.","Hold this teaching point: Two or three precise sentences are stronger than a long list of adjectives.","Worked comparison: Choose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence."],"teach3":["Focus on check a reader can infer the intended feeling.","Hold this teaching point: Read your scene aloud and ask what feeling seems most likely. If several feelings fit equally, add one clearer clue.","Worked comparison: Add a clue that distinguishes them, such as a jump back for fear versus a wide grin for pleasant surprise."],"write":["Use the independent instructions, not the model words.","Give an original response and the reason behind it.","Independent reminder: Write three short original scenes (two sentences each) that show worry, surprise and relief without using those three words. Underline the exact actions that su"]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use showing with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify telling and point to the words or events that support it.","I can practise clue independently on a new prompt.","I can notice inference needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying showing independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to name the feeling, then remove the label?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without using that word.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nRewrite \"Tom was angry\" using actions only.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nExample: \"Tom slammed his pencil onto the desk and stared at the torn page.\" The actions imply anger.","explain":"Telling names the emotion; showing lets the reader infer it from actions, body language and surroundings.\n\nTelling names the emotion; showing lets the reader infer it from actions, body language and surroundings. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nReplacing angry with mad still tells; show observable evidence. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Name the feeling, then remove the label","visual":[{"alt":"Telling names fear while showing uses gripping the railing as evidence.","caption":"Show observable actions so a reader can infer the feeling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tells</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Pia was frightened</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shows</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Pia gripped the railing</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.","who":"juno"},{"text":"So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without u Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.\nPip: So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without u Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying telling independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to choose useful sensory or physical clues?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nFor excitement I write \"Amira bounced on her toes and checked the window again.\" The bouncing and repeated checking suggest anticipation.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nWrite two clues that could show worry at a bus stop.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nChecking the timetable twice and biting a lip are useful clues; naming the bus colour is not.","explain":"Not every detail helps. Choose actions a reader can picture that point towards one feeling.\n\nNot every detail helps. Choose actions a reader can picture that point towards one feeling. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nA smile alone can mean many feelings; add a second confirming clue. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Choose useful sensory or physical clues","visual":[{"alt":"A smile alone versus bouncing and checking a window as excitement clues.","caption":"One ambiguous clue is weaker than two confirming actions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Weak clue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">smiled only</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stronger clues</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">bounced and checked the window</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying clue independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to keep the scene short and clear?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"Leo''s hands shook as he opened the envelope. He exhaled when he saw his name.\" Relief is clear without naming it.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nTrim a draft that lists five adjectives for surprise into two clear sentences.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nChoose two concrete actions; delete repeated describing words that do not add evidence.","explain":"Two or three precise sentences are stronger than a long list of adjectives.\n\nTwo or three precise sentences are stronger than a long list of adjectives. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nPiling synonyms for the emotion is still telling. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Keep the scene short and clear"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying inference independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to check a reader can infer the intended feeling?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nMy draft about a lost dog could sound sad or calm. I add \"He wiped his eyes on his sleeve\" so sadness is clearer.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nImprove a scene that could mean either surprise or fear.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nAdd a clue that distinguishes them, such as a jump back for fear versus a wide grin for pleasant surprise.","explain":"Read your scene aloud and ask what feeling seems most likely. If several feelings fit equally, add one clearer clue.\n\nRead your scene aloud and ask what feeling seems most likely. If several feelings fit equally, add one clearer clue. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not claim the feeling is obvious if your clues support more than one reading. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Check a reader can infer the intended feeling"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 4, Lesson 2: Show, don''t tell","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"I DO — \"Pia was frightened\" tells. \"Pia gripped the railing as the floorboards creaked\" shows clues that suggest fear without using that word.\n\nNow look for the exact evidence in the words or actions. For excitement I write \"Amira bounced on her toes and checked the window again.\" The bouncing and repeated checking suggest anticipation.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Telling names fear while showing uses gripping the railing as evidence.","caption":"Show observable actions so a reader can infer the feeling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150